+# Verifier mode for the bridge: caller nonce in, presentation out
+
+Status: implemented and tested offline (`verifier-service/src/bridge.rs`,
+`verifier-service/tests/bridge_http.rs`). Consumed by the Nachweis bridge
+(`nachweis-app/service`, `VERIFIER_URL` set), which creates the request, lets
+the wallet answer it, fetches the verified presentation and proves the
+statement subset in the zkVM.
+
+## What it is
+
+The default flow (`GET /`) mints its own UUID nonce and keeps, after
+verification, only the parsed `VerifiedPid` (disclosed claims, vct, holder
+binding). The bridge needs two things the default flow does not offer:
+
+1. the nonce has to be ITS nonce, `hex(sha256(address20 || challenge))`, so the
+   KB-JWT the wallet signs is bound to the EVM address (same formula as the
+   blind relay, see `docs/blind-relay.md`, "Nonce formula");
+2. the raw SD-JWT presentation afterwards, because the zkVM statement
+   re-verifies the issuer signature, the disclosures, `sd_hash` and the
+   KB-JWT nonce binding itself.
+
+Verifier mode is the default path with two JSON doors for exactly that. The
+request is the same registered minimal ask (`given_name`, `family_name`,
+`age_equal_or_over.18`), signed with the same registrar leaf, encrypted to the
+service's own key, answered on the same `POST /response/:id`, verified by the
+same code (decrypt, issuer signature, trust anchor if configured, status list
+if configured, vct, holder binding, nonce, aud, sd_hash, freshness). Only the
+nonce source and what is kept afterwards differ.
+
+## Privacy note, stated plainly
+
+In verifier mode the verifier decrypts the presentation, so it sees the
+plaintext PID claims (as it does on the landing-page path today), and it
+additionally keeps the presentation string in memory and hands it to the
+bridge. Verifier and bridge are meant to run on the SAME host; the endpoint is
+not designed to be exposed to a third party, and `GET /result/:id` carries no
+authentication beyond the session id (a random UUID). Two consequences:
+
+- Do not put `GET /result/:id` on a public origin. Bind the verifier to
+  localhost, or front it with a reverse proxy that exposes `/request/:id` and
+  `/response/:id` to the wallet and nothing else.
+- `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION=false` keeps only the parsed summary; the
+  endpoint then answers `verified` with `presentation_withheld` instead of
+  `presentation`, and the bridge's verifier mode cannot work.
+
+The blind relay (`docs/blind-relay.md`) is the privacy-preserving alternative:
+there the client holds the decryption key and the server stores the wallet's
+JWE unopened, so the server never sees the presentation. It costs the client a
+key pair and the decrypt-and-verify step; verifier mode costs the user the
+verifier's view of the plaintext. The two modes coexist on one service; a
+session belongs to exactly one of them, decided by which endpoint created it.
+
+## Sequence
+
+```text
+bridge (same host)                 verifier-service                  wallet (phone)
+-------------------------------    ------------------------------    --------------------
+challenge = 32 random bytes
+nonce = hex(sha256(addr20 || challenge))
+POST /request { nonce [, redirect_uri] } ->
+                                   validate nonce (16..128, [A-Za-z0-9_-])
+                                   409 if a PENDING session has this nonce
+                                   build JAR: nonce, direct_post.jwt,
+                                     client_metadata.jwks = [own enc key],
+                                     dcql_query = registered minimal ask
+                                   sign with the registrar leaf (x509_hash)
+                                   bridge table: {nonce, pending}
+                                <- 201 { session_id, request_uri,
+                                         openid4vp_uri, nonce, result_url }
+render QR / open openid4vp_uri  - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - ->  scan / open
+                                   GET /request/:id                  <-
+                                   -> JAR (application/oauth-authz-req+jwt)
+                                                                     verify JAR, consent,
+                                                                     vp_token + KB-JWT
+                                                                       (nonce, aud=client_id),
+                                                                     JWE to the service key
+                                   POST /response/:id                <-
+                                     response=<JWE>
+                                   decrypt, verify (as on GET /)
+                                   store {VerifiedPid, presentation string}
+                                   bridge table: completed (nonce free again)
+                                   -> 200 { status: verified, inspect
+                                            [, redirect_uri] }
+                                      or 422 { status: rejected, reason }
+GET /result/:id (poll)          ->
+                                <- 200 { status: pending }
+                                   ...
+                                <- 200 { status: verified, presentation,
+                                         verified_pid, nonce }
+native statement + zkVM proof over `presentation`
+```
+
+## HTTP surface
+
+### `POST /request`
+
+Body (JSON):
+
+```json
+{
+  "nonce": "538cc3ecfb6325d0f4adb5c784a8caac93bbac1cd69b10701b210ff8e66f84ab",
+  "redirect_uri": "nachweis://return"
+}
+```
+
+- `nonce` (required): 16 to 128 characters of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`. Lowercase hex
+  (the bridge), base64url and UUIDs all fit; anything else is 400. The value
+  goes into the signed request object verbatim.
+- `redirect_uri` (optional): an absolute URL, custom scheme allowed. Returned
+  verbatim to the wallet in the answer to a VERIFIED `POST /response/:id`
+  (OpenID4VP 1.0, section 8.2), so a same-device flow comes back to the app.
+
+Answer `201 Created`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "session_id": "3f2c…",
+  "session": "3f2c…",
+  "request_uri": "https://verifier.example/request/3f2c…",
+  "openid4vp_uri": "openid4vp://?client_id=x509_hash%3A…&request_uri=…",
+  "authorization_request": "openid4vp://?client_id=x509_hash%3A…&request_uri=…",
+  "nonce": "538cc3ec…84ab",
+  "result_url": "https://verifier.example/result/3f2c…"
+}
+```
+
+`session` and `authorization_request` are the names the bridge client reads;
+`session_id` and `openid4vp_uri` match `POST /relay/request`. The pairs carry
+identical values. The bridge session id IS the verifier session id, so the app
+polls one id.
+
+Errors: `400` (nonce shape, relative `redirect_uri`, unparseable body),
+`409` when another session created through this endpoint still waits for its
+wallet response with the same nonce (the body names that session). A nonce is
+released once its session is verified or rejected. Landing-page sessions
+(`GET /`) are not in the table, so their UUID nonces are not checked against
+caller nonces.
+
+### `GET /result/:id`
+
+Always `200` for a session this endpoint knows; the `status` field carries
+the state. `400` for a non-UUID id, `404` for a session with no outcome that
+was not created through `POST /request` (a landing-page session before its
+response, a relay or ZK session, an unknown id).
+
+Pending:
+
+```json
+{ "status": "pending", "session_id": "3f2c…", "nonce": "538cc3ec…84ab" }
+```
+
+Verified:
+
+```json
+{
+  "status": "verified",
+  "session_id": "3f2c…",
+  "nonce": "538cc3ec…84ab",
+  "presentation": "<issuer JWT>~<disclosure>~…~<KB-JWT>",
+  "verified_pid": { "vct": "urn:eudi:pid:de:1", "view": { … }, "holder_bound": true }
+}
+```
+
+`presentation` is the SD-JWT VC presentation exactly as it appeared in the
+decrypted `vp_token`: issuer JWT, the disclosures the wallet chose, and the
+KB-JWT, `~`-separated, byte for byte. A consumer that re-verifies it sees the
+same bytes this verifier verified. With `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION=false`
+the key is absent and `presentation_withheld` explains why.
+
+`verified_pid` is the same parsed summary the inspector view uses. `nonce` is
+`null` for a landing-page session (its UUID nonce is not tracked).
+
+Rejected:
+
+```json
+{
+  "status": "rejected",
+  "session_id": "3f2c…",
+  "nonce": "538cc3ec…84ab",
+  "error": "KB-JWT nonce does not match the request",
+  "reason": "KB-JWT nonce does not match the request"
+}
+```
+
+`error` and `reason` carry the same text; the bridge client reads `reason`.
+
+### Unchanged
+
+`GET /`, `GET /request/:id`, `GET /inspect/:id`, the ZK endpoints
+(`GET /zk/result/:id` included) and the relay endpoints behave as before. The
+only change on the shared response path is that a verified default-path
+session now also keeps its presentation string (in memory, same lifetime as
+the parsed result, gated by the flag).
+
+## Configuration
+
+| variable | default | effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION` | `true` | keep the decrypted presentation of a verified session and serve it from `GET /result/:id`; `false` keeps the parsed summary only |
+
+Nothing else is needed: the endpoints are always mounted, the nonce table is
+in memory, and sessions live as long as the process (no pruning, as for the
+relay table and the results map today).
+
+## Limits
+
+- No pruning: the bridge table and the results map grow with the number of
+  sessions until restart. Same as the rest of the service today.
+- One outcome per session: a second wallet POST to a completed session is
+  refused at the openid4vp session-store layer, and the first outcome stands.
+- `GET /result/:id` is unauthenticated beyond the session id. See the privacy
+  note: same host only.
+- Transport-level failures on the wallet's POST (a malformed form body, a
+  session the store refuses) are not recorded as a rejection; the session stays
+  `pending` and the wallet may retry. The bridge's own poll timeout (10 min)
+  bounds the wait.
